                        "num": "1",
                        "title": "Gross Vehicle Weight Rating (GVWR)",
                        "intro": "Most standard dry van trailers have a GVWR of 65,000 to 68,000 pounds. This determines how much total weight the trailer can legally carry, including its own weight. If you consistently haul heavier freight, this number directly impacts your profitability."
                      },
                      {
                        "points": [],
                        "num": "2",
                        "title": "Trailer Weight",
                        "intro": "The trailer weight is the empty weight of the trailer. Lighter trailers mean more payload capacity. Modern composite panel designs and high-strength steel components help reduce trailer weight without sacrificing durability."
                      },
                      {
                        "points": [],
                        "num": "3",
                        "title": "Floor Type",
                        "intro": "Most dry vans use hardwood floors, laminated floors, or aluminum floor systems. If you regularly haul heavy palletized freight, floor strength ratings matter. A 16-inch or 12-inch cross-member spacing can significantly impact durability."
                      },
                      {
                        "points": [],
                        "num": "4",
                        "title": "Door Type",
                        "intro": "You'll typically choose between swing doors and roll-up doors. Swing doors are more common and lighter, while roll-up doors are convenient for dock operations."
